    "name": "Density Buckets",
    "text": "2\nDensity Buckets\nInstructions\n\nMake a spreadsheet with two columns, or use this template:\n\nColumn 1: High Density\n\nColumn 2: Low Density\n\nComplete an LR section* using any workout, but with one small adjustment: Write your Mission in either the High-Density or Low-Density column in the spreadsheet.\n\nChoose which column to place your Mission into based on how information-dense your Mission is.\n\nThe High Density column is where you write your high information density Missions.\n\nA Mission with high information density will use words and concepts from the stimulus directly without referential phrasing, glazing over the details, and characterizing information (\"all that stuff\") to avoid having to say it directly.\n\nExample Mission in the High-Density Bucket: Whether there actually is more food in the ocean tropics versus the freshwater tropics\n\nThe Low Density column is where you write your low information density Missions.\n\nA Mission with low information density will use referential phrasing (\"this,\" \"that,\" \"it,\" \"they,\" \"the conclusion,\" etc.) to gloss over the direct words and concepts from the stimulus, omit the details like specific nouns and verbs, and group information together with broad characterizations (\"good things\").\n\nExample Mission on the Same Question in the Low-Density Bucket: Food is important\n\nAfter you complete the section, review the spreadsheet to note which questions produced Low-Density Missions.\n\nReturn to the questions that produced Low-Density Missions the next day and try to make a new higher density Mission before you Camo the section.\n\nSEE FULL WORKOUT DETAILS",
